투자는 항상 어려움. 어떤 시기가 찾아오는지도 알기 어렵고 어떤 곳이 버블이 있는지 없는지 알기가 굉장히 어려움. 이번에는 
반도체쪽이 버블이 있다고 사람들이 이야기해도 코로나 시기와는 다르게 펀더멘탈이 충분히 있었고 적정주가와 거리가 멀게 
많이 쌈. 아직도 싸다고 생각함. 오히려 그때 외면받는 소프트웨어가 지금은 많이 올라가고있음. 

1. 많이 싸다고 생각해도 사람들과 시장의 속도가 맞지 않으면 확 떨어질 수도 있음. 
2. 그러면 사람들이 뺀 돈을 오히려 돈이 많이 들어가있지 않은 굉장히 싸다고 생각하는 회사에 투자함 (빅테크, 소프트웨어, 비트코인(조금 예외) 등이 이번에 그랬음)
3. 반대로 생각하는게 아닌 오히려 욕심이 없는 곳에 투자하는게 적절하다고 볼 수도 있지만 그 흐름을 못 먹는건 꽤 손실이 큼. 적정 비율로 적당히 굴리는게 항상 중요 .
4. 지수투자나 비트코인 등 오히려 쉽게 움직이는 곳 투자가 더 쉽다고 생각이 듬. -> 자동매매 공부중
5. 항상 부분매도 전량매도 등 감정에 휩쓸리지 않고 해야할듯
6. 나는 아직 반도체가 많이 싸다고 생각함. 그러면 지금이 기회? 경제상황이 많이 안도와주고 있지만 조금 시간이 걸려도 괜찮을듯. 
7. 그렇다면 지금 소프트웨어의 부분매도와 반도체 추가 매수를 생각해야하나? 아니면 현금 보유를 하고 반도체가 오를때 들어가야하나 고민중 아니면 그냥 유지


Investing remains inherently difficult — it is nearly impossible to anticipate market cycles or to identify with confidence where asset bubbles may be forming.
While many market participants argue that the AI and semiconductor sectors are currently overvalued, I would contend that, unlike the speculative excess observed during the COVID-era rally, 
these companies are underpinned by solid fundamentals and demonstrated earnings growth. In my view, current valuations remain well below fair value, and the sector — spanning AI infrastructure as well as memory and NAND semiconductors
— continues to present an attractive investment opportunity. Notably, software equities, which were largely overlooked during that earlier period, have since experienced a significant rally.

1. Even when valuations appear attractive, a mismatch in timing between market sentiment and fundamentals can still trigger a sharp decline.
2. In such cases, capital withdrawn from overheated names tends to rotate into stocks perceived as significantly undervalued and relatively under-owned 
— as seen recently with big tech, software, and, to a lesser extent, Bitcoin.
3. One could argue that it is more prudent to invest where sentiment is not yet crowded, rather than positioning against the prevailing trend — 
but missing that momentum can also result in substantial opportunity cost. Maintaining an appropriate allocation across both remains essential.
4. I've come to believe that index investing and assets like Bitcoin, which tend to move more predictably with the broader trend, may actually be easier to trade systematically — 
hence my current focus on studying automated trading strategies.
5. In any case, decisions around partial or full liquidation should be made without being swayed by emotion.
6. I still believe semiconductors remain significantly undervalued. Does that mean now is the opportunity? Macroeconomic conditions remain unfavorable at present, 
but I think it's reasonable to allow more time for this thesis to play out.
7. That raises the question of whether I should now consider partially trimming software positions to add to semiconductors, or instead hold cash and wait to enter semiconductors on strength — 
or simply maintain current positioning as is.
